1. Introduction The ever aggravation of energy reduction and global warming issues makes a huge call for not only furious development of clean, sustainable, surrogate energies and forced emission of various gases responsible for global warming, rather also highly precocious energy storage devices with appealing storing and delivering capabilities [1–3]. Supercapacitors (also termed as electrochemical capacitors) with merits of offering high power density and excellent cyclic performances are probably the most pivotal next generation energy storage device ranging from portable electronics to electrics as well as hybrid vehicles [4,5]. On the other hand, direct methanol fuel cells (DMFCs) have also acknowledged to be auspicious power source for portable electronic device and hybrid vehicles, on account of their copious raw materials, conversion efficiency, minimal emission, as well as low operational temperature and high power density [6,7]. Depending on the charge storage mechanisms, supercapacitors (SCs) can be primarily categorized into pseudocapacitors and electric double layer capacitors (EDLCs). Among these, pseudocapacitors possess the high energy density and store energy through faradic reactions (redox reactions) [5,8–12]. In contrast, energy reservoir in EDLCs primarily generates via reversible ion adsorption/desorption at electrode interface and possess high rate performance as well as enhanced cyclic life span but minimal energy density [13,14]. Consequently, the hybridization of pseudocapacitors and battery-type materials with EDLCs materials provide novel hybrid supercapacitors (HSCs) assembly, which explore the dual benefits of batteries (having high energy density but low power density) and supercapacitors (high power density but low energy density) in a single device [4,12,15–19]. Nowadays, the research related to modish and lowcost designation of HSC have taken prominent attention by virtue of their surplus energy and power density, rapid charge discharge phenomena, and ultra-long life span [20–22]. Nonetheless, the practical implementation of HSCs have been still shielded by shrinkage of scrupulous designation of electrode materials with prosperous electrochemical features in terms of high energy and power sources, as well as capacity/capacitance, rate performance and cyclic stability. Therefore, construction of multifunctional, cost effective electrode materials having excellent electrochemical activity is urgently needed to develop precocious HSCs and DMFCs. In recent years, poly-phase transition metal oxides (PTMOs) encompassed of more than two metal cations with appealing nanostructured features have been scrutinized as the highranked auspicious electrode materials for supercapacitors and electrocatalytic activity owing to their arrays of preference including enriched electronic conductivity, large potential window, high energy density as well as remarkable electrochemical activities compared with carbon and conductive polymer derived electrode materials [23,24]. The appreciated electrochemical performances of PTMOs based electrode predominantly associated with compositional parameters, in which synergistic effect of multiple valence states of metal cations co-exist to uplift the overall electrochemical performance [25,26]. Among the bundles of PTMOs, zinc-nickel– cobalt oxides (ZNCO) possess supreme priority while constructing the electrode materials for HSC, enormously thankful to uppermost electronic conductivity of zinc $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ metal, multiple valence state of nickel (Ni) and cobalt (Co) along with wide range of operating voltage imparting magnificent electrochemical activities, intensified electronic conductivity and higher capacitance/capacity [23,25,27]. Primarily, poly-phase metal oxides with plenteous structural defects can be obtained by immersion of polymorphous metal ions during the fabrication of electrode materials. Furthermore, the combined effect of Zn, Ni and Co oxides in alkaline environment exhibits immense pseudocapacitive (battery-type) behavior with rapid redox reactions, symmetric nature of charge/ discharge curve, and towering capacity due to the gentle ion penetration via diffusion process compared to its bulk assembly [28,29]. Instead, it has been demonstrated that low electrical conductivity and minimum surface area for PTMOs based electrode materials are still in the verge of seeking practicability for energy storage applications. To get rid of these shortcomings, construction of ZNCO nanomaterials onto reduced graphene oxide (rGO) decorated three-dimensional (3D) condcutive substrate could be successful approach to boost the electrochemical performance of ZNCO based electrode materials for SCs and electrocatalysts [30,31]. Among the various carbon materials, rGO possess high electrical conductivity and large surface area, which ameliorate to upgrade electronic conductivity, capacity as well as durability of electrode materials [31]. Moreover, the supercapacitive performance can be elevated by using binder-free electrode composed of ZNCO onto rGO coated 3D Ni foam. The binder free 3D Ni scaffold not only serve as current collector rather it also support for uniform growth of ZNCO nanomaterials and enormously favors the accessibility of electroactive materials towards the current collector [24,26].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), an alluring caste of solid crystalline materials, have earned considerable attention in diverse of applications, including sensing, catalysis, drug delivery, fuel cells, supercapacitors, batteries, owing to their inherent porosity features, ordered channel, plentiful electroactive components and exceptionally mammoth surface area and desirable functionalities [32–36]. Specifically, the MOFs derived nanomaterials regain their original porous structure conserving pore connectivity and surface hydrophilicity, which is assumed to be highly beneficial to elevate diffusion and transportation of electrolyte ions as well as tenders sheer electroactive sites to achieve remarkable electrochemical performances [33,37,38]. Among the deck of various MOFs, zeolitic imidazolate framework (M-Im-M, where M represents metal cation and Im signifies imidazolate linker) with 2D structure possess suitable pores, large open-void-surface, shortened chargetransport distance with rapid charge–discharge, when employed as electrode materials for SCs [39,40]. For instance, recently, Yang et al. prepared $\mathsf{C o S}_{\mathrm{x}}$ quantum dots embedded Zn-Ni-Co-LDH nanosheets onto carbon cloth via MOF derived in-situ vulcanization process. The as-constructed electrode showed an outstanding specific capacitance value of $2504\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ( $350.6\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}},$ at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ [41]. Similarly, Asadi et al. prepared cobalt based MOF (ZIF-67) as a non-precious catalyst with outstanding catalytic activity and remarkable stability towards methanol electro-oxidation [42].
In the present work, we introduced MOF derived hierarchical Zn-Ni-Co triangular nanosheet arrays on rGO decorated Ni foam as binder free electrode for supercapacitors and electroxidation of methanol. When scrutinized as the cathode material for supercapacitor, the as-designed ZNCO nanosheet electrode displayed excellent specific capacitance $(267\:\mathrm{mAh}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ ) with ultra-high rate performance $(83.82\%$ at $50\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ ) due to the strong coupling effects of each individual components. The as-prepared ZNCO nanostructured also exhibited remarkable catalytic activity towards the electro-oxidation of methanol. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first attempt in which ZNCO was tested for electroxidation of methanol in alkaline medium. For negative electrode, rGO decorated MOF derived porous carbon (rGO-MDPC) was prepared and sandwiched with hierarchical ZNCO nanomaterial to fabricate HSC device with an extended potential window of $1.5{\mathrm{V}}.$ The HSC device exhibited a higher energy density of 61.25 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of 750 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at the current density of $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , and capacitance retention of $94.52\%$ of its initial specific capacitance even after 6,000 cycles suggesting its long-term durability. These subordinate outcomes favor convictive strategy of using cost-effective raw materials, and the rational fabrication of HSC device proposed in the present work could be cardinal reference for diverse of materials for designation of high performance energy storage and conversion devices.
## 2. Experimental part
## 2.1. Materials and chemicals
Graphite powder $(\geq99.99\%$ ), $N$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP, $99.5\%$ , poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF, average $\mathsf{M}_{\mathsf{w}}=534,000\mathrm{)}$ , zinc-nitrate hexahydrate $\left(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O},98\%\right)$ , nickel nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0,$ $297\%$ , cobalt nitrate hexahydrate (Co $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $98\%$ , 2-methylimidazole $\mathrm{\tilde{C}}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , $99\%$ ), were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Hydrochloric acid (HCl, $35\mathrm{-}37\%$ , methanol $C_{\mathrm{H}_{3}}0\mathrm{H}$ , $299\%$ ), Potassium hydroxide (KOH, $\geq99.5\%$ , ethanol ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{5}{\mathsf{O H}}$ , $299\%$ ), carbon black ${\tt(\geq99.99\%)}$ , were purchased from Daejung Chemical Co., Ltd., South Korea. All the reagents were of analytical grade and used without further modification.
## 2.2. Deposition of reduced graphene oxide (rGO) onto Ni foam
Prior to usage, Ni foam was cleaned with deionized water (DI), ethanol and acetone subsequently under sonication chased by vacuum drying furnace at $60^{\circ}C$ for $^{6\mathrm{{h}}}$ . Graphene oxide (GO) was prepared from graphite powder by modified Hummer’s method [43]. The decoration of rGO onto cleaned Ni foam was carried out by chemical reduction method. Briefly, $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of GO was dispersed in $40\mathrm{ml}$ of DI water for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ followed by addition of $60\mathrm{mg}$ of ascorbic acid with continuous sonication for another $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Next, a slice of cleaned Ni foam (NF) was kept inside GO dispersion and heated under water bath at $90^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ to get ${\mathrm{rGO@NF}}$ .
## 2.3. Preparation of zinc-cobalt MOF (Zn-Co-MOF)
Firstly, ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ and $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ in the molar ratio of 1:1 was dissolved in $40~\mathrm{ml}$ DI water and labelled as solution A. Similarly, solution $\mathtt{B}$ was prepared by dissolving 16 mmol of 2 methyl imidazole (2-MiM) into same volume of DI water. Then, a slice of as-fabricated ${\mathrm{rGO@NF}}$ was dipped into the solution A for 10 min and solution B was added into solution A with continuous stirring for few minutes. After ageing for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the purplecolored sample onto NF was collected, washed with copious water and ethanol followed by drying at $60~^{\circ}C$ overnight to get Zn-Co$\mathsf{M O F@r G O-N F}$ . For comparison, we have also prepared CoMOF@rGO-NF by similar approach without addition of Zn $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$
