## 2.5. Instruments for the characterizations of as-prepared materials
The crystalline features of the as-fabricated materials were diagnosed via Rigaku X-ray diffractometer (Tokyo, Japan). The microstructure and in-depth surface morphological aspects of materials were examined through Field Emission Scanning Electron Microscopy (FE-SEM, S-7400, Hitachi, Tokyo, Japan) equipped with Energy dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy (EDX) as well as HighResolution Transmission Electron Microscopy (HR-TEM, JEOL, Tokyo, Japan). The elemental oxidation state and chemical composition of the as-prepared materials were investigated by X-ray photo electron spectroscopy (XPS, ESCALAB250, Al Ka radiation). The Raman spectra were collected by a Fourier transform infrared-Raman spectrometer (RFS-100 S, Bruker, Hamburg, Germany). The Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were recorded by using an ABB Bomen MB100 Spectrometer (Bomen, Canada). Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) and Barrett-JoynerHalenda (BJH) analysis for specific surface area (SSA), pore volume and pore size distribution (PSD) were investigated by a $\mathsf{N}_{2}/77$ K adsorption–desorption experiment with an adsorption analyzer (BELSORP-max, Japan).
## 2.6. Electrochemical analysis for three electrode system and HSC device
The electrochemical investigations for as-fabricated electrode materials were inspected in 2 M KOH electrolyte at room temperature in a conventional three-electrode cell configuration assuming as-designed materials as working electrodes, $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ as reference electrode and platinum $(\mathrm{Pt})$ wire as auxiliary electrode respectively. The pertinent cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) of all electrodes were supervised on VersaSTAT 4 electrochemical workstation under room temperature (RT) condition. The as-fabricated cathode materials were directly used as working electrodes while anode materials were prepared by coating the homogeneous slurry composed of $80~\mathrm{wt\%}$ active materials, $10\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ carbon black and $10\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ PVDF organic binder mixed in NMP solvent onto a slice of Ni foam with an area of $1\times1~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . Moreover, the HSC device was fabricated and employed in two electrode configuration, in which ZNCO acts as positive electrode, rGO-MDPC as negative electrode, cellulose filter paper as the separator and $2\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH as electrolyte. Herein, it should be noted that, the masses of positive and negative electrodes are crucial parameters during assembly of HSC device, so as to achieve optimal electrochemical performance. The mass loading ratio $\mathrm{(m_{+}/m_{-})}$ was predicted by computing the charges on solitary electrodes as provided by the following equation:
$$
{\mathfrak{q}}^{+}={\mathfrak{q}}^{-}
$$
Now, the amount of charge (q) acquired by electrodes with respect to their specific capacity $(\mathsf C_{\mathsf S})$ can be obtained as:
$$
\mathsf{q}=\mathsf{C}_{s}\times\Delta\mathsf{V}\times\mathsf{m}
$$
where $\Delta\mathsf{V}$ and m represents the working potential window and mass of the active material respectively.
On the basis of Eqs. (1) and (2) the mass ratio $\left(\mathfrak{m}_{+}/\mathfrak{m}_{-}\right)$ can be expressed as:
$$
\mathrm{m_{+}/m_{-}}=\mathsf C_{s-}\times\Delta\mathsf V_{-}/\mathsf C_{s+}\times\Delta\mathsf V_{+}
$$
Herein, based on the respective $C_{S}$ values and operating potential voltage, the ideal mass loading ratio for positive and negative electrode $\mathrm{(m_{+}/m_{-})}$ was evaluated to be 0.69.
The positive and negative electrodes were examined through CV measurements within the working potential window of $-0.2$ to $0.7~\mathrm{V}$ and $-1.0$ to $0.0\mathrm{\V}$ (vs $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}_{,}^{\prime}$ ), respectively, at the scan rates of $5{-}200\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ . Similarly, GCD test was performed within the range of 0–0.45 V for the positive electrode at the current density of $1{-}50\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . Instead, the GCD test for negative electrode was examined at the potential window of $-1.0$ to $0.0\mathrm{V}$ at the current density of $1-20\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ . Also, the electrochemical evaluation for the as-assembled HSC device was conducted within the potential range of $1.5~\mathrm{V}.$ The EIS spectrum of the prepared materials and HSC device was operated at $10~\mathrm{mV}$ amplitude of AC voltage in the frequency range of $1-100,000\mathrm{Hz}$ . The specific capacity $(\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{S}})$ values for various electrodes and ASC device were calculated via following relation [3,44–46]:
$$
\mathsf C_{\mathsf s}=2\mathsf I\times\int\mathsf{V}\mathsf{d}\mathsf{t}_{\mathrm{d}}/\mathsf{m}\times\mathsf V\times3600
$$
where $\mathrm{\Delta}J\mathrm{\nabla{dt_{d}}}$ denotes the integral area of the discharge curve, I signifies the discharge current (A), m indicates the mass of active materials, $\mathrm{dt_{d}}$ signifies discharge time (s) and $\mathsf{V}$ is the operating potential voltage (V). Furthermore, the energy density (E, Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}.$ and power density $(\mathrm{P},\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}),$ ) of the sandwiched ASC device were estimated via following equations:
$$
\mathrm{E}=2\mathrm{I}\times\int\mathrm{Vdt}_{\mathrm{d}}/\mathrm{m}\times3.6
$$
$$
\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{E}/\Delta\mathrm{t}\times3600
$$
where $\mathrm{\Delta}f\mathrm{\nabla}\mathsf{d t}_{\mathrm{{d}}}$ signifies the integral area of the discharge curve and V and $\Delta{\sf t}$ represents the operating voltage (V), discharge time (s) respectively.
## 2.7. Electrocatalytic activity test
The electrocatalytic activity and pertinent stability of the ZNCO electrode material for methanol $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}0\mathrm{H})$ oxidation were demonstrated via cyclic voltammetry (CV), chronoamperometry (CA) and EIS tests at RT using 1 M KOH with or without 0.5 M $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ electrolytes.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology, chemical composition, crystalline phase, and porosity investigation
The synthetic approach of triangular shaped hierarchical ZNCO nanosheet arrays on rGO coated NF was schematically elaborated in Fig. 1. Firstly, rGO were deposited onto Ni foam by chemical reduction method. Subsequently, green and cost-effective, solution based method was employed to grow solid leaves like ZnCo-MOFs. During fabrication of Zn-Co-MOFs, $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $C o^{2+}$ radicals were supposed to be bonded with negatively charged (aroused through delocalized and loan pair of electron) N-atom. Afterwards, Zn-Co-MOFs were transformed into Zn-Ni-CO-LDH by hydrolysis controlled ion exchange and etching process in which $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ ion plays pivotal role to etch Zn-Co-MOFs and uplift the deposition of Zn-Ni-Co-LDH nanosheets [47,48]. Finally, NiZn-Co-LDH precursors were annealed to get triangular shaped hierarchical hollow Zn-Ni-Co-oxide decorated with branched like nanosheets arrays onto rGO coated Ni foam which was directly used as working electrode for both SCs and electro-oxidation of methanol.
The elemental oxidation states and pertinent chemical composition of the ZNCO nanomaterials onto rGO/NF were scrutinized by XPS technique. As shown in XPS survey spectrum (Fig. S3), the asprepared nanomaterials show intense Zn 2p, Ni 2p, Co 2p, O 1s and C 1s peaks, implying the presence of Zn, Ni, Co, O and C elements. The high-resolution spectrum of $Z_{\mathsf{n}}\mathsf{2p}$ provides two primary peaks located at 1021.38 and $1044.35\mathrm{eV}$ , ascribed to the Zn $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Zn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ for $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ valence states (Fig. 2b) [47]. Similarly, the highresolution Ni 2p spectrum (Fig. 2c) reveals two prominent peaks attested with binding energies of 855.04 and 872.67 eV for Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ doublets, respectively, conjoined with other two shake-up satellites peaks (denoted as Sat.) affirming the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ oxidation state [39,40]. Fig. 2d depicts the high-resolution Co 2p spectrum, which can be fitted into two doublets $(\mathsf C02\mathsf p_{3/2}$ and Co $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}\mathrm{.}$ as well as two shake-up satellites, implying the coexistence of ${\dot{C}}0^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ oxidation states [53]. The two imperative peaks centered at 781.17 and $795.73\mathrm{eV}$ were assigned to the $C0^{2+}$ oxidation state, while the peak positioned at $779.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ reveals the existence of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ valence state [28,29,53]. Moreover, the highresolution spectrum of O 1s (Fig. 2e) can be deconvulated into two prominent peaks positioned at 529.58 and $531.36\mathrm{eV}$ suggesting the presence of metal oxygen (M-O-M) linkage with the $0^{2-}$ valence state [23]. Additionally, the high-resolution spectrum for C 1s (Fig. 2f) displays the solitary peak escorted by other secondary peaks positioned at the binding energies of 284.98 and $287.17\ \mathrm{eV}$ assuring the successful decoration of rGO onto NF during fabrication of ZNCO nanostructure onto rGO/NF [53]. Therefore, all these results were large enough to proclaim the successful growth of ZNCO nanomaterials onto rGO coated Ni foam by a simple fabrication technique.
The morphology of as-prepared materials plays pivotal role while investigating the electrochemical activity. Thus, the surface morphology and microstructure of various nanomaterials were studied under FESEM analysis. Primarily, the FESEM images of Ni foam before and after deposition of rGO was clearly displayed in
Fig. S4. The FESEM images of Co-MOF (Fig. 3a and the inset) revealed the solid and smooth nanowall arrays microstructure, which were uniformly grown over the conductive rGO-NF via solution based co-precipitation method. Fig. 3b and its inset shows the FESEM images of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (CO), which clearly demonstrates the conversion of thick wall of Co-MOF into much thinner nanowall after calcination process. Fig. 3c and d manifest the FESEM images for Ni-Co-LDH and NCO nanostructures respectively. As demonstrated in Fig. 3c (inset shows the magnified image), Ni-Co-LDH nanosheets were appeared as dense coral-like aggregated structure composed of large number of void space. The solid nanowall arrays of CO-MOF after etching process were transformed into rough and porous nanosheets in the form of branched structure of Ni-Co-LDH. Instead, after calcination process the aggregated Ni-Co-LDH was transfigured into triangular shaped nanomaterials maintaining original shape of MOF decorated with ultrathin nanosheets of NCO. Fig. 3e and f depicts the FESEM images of Zn-Co-MOF and Zn-Ni-Co-LDH respectively. As revealed by Fig. 3e (inset provides magnified image), the nature of Zn-Co-MOF is quite different as compared to pure Co-MOF and appeared in the form of solid, smooth and uniformly grown nanoleave arrays. Also, after the successful etching and ion exchange process, smooth surface of 2D ZnCo-MOF was completely converted into unevenly distributed nanosheet stacked in the form of flower like structure of Zn-NiCo-LDH. The as-obtained Zn-Ni-Co-LDH was not appeared as agglomerated nanostructure rather do not possess original shape of MOFs. Nevertheless, after calcination, the original and appealing shape of 2D MOFs comprised of unique branched-like hierarchical, ultrathin ZNCO nanosheets were achieved (Fig. $3\mathrm{g}$ -inset shows the magnified image). The elemental states relating to formation of ZNCO nanosheets were investigated by EDX technique. The elemental mapping (Fig. 3h) and EDX inspection (Fig. S5), apparently diagnosed the presence of anticipated elements like Ni, Co, Zn, C and O in their proper ratio, implying the prosperous fabrication of ZNCO nanomaterials on rGO coated Ni foam. Such ultrathin nanosheets with porous nanostructure were supposed to possess high-surface area which enhance the accessibility of ions penetration and curtain the ion–electron roadways to enhance the electrochemical activity towards the excellent supercapacitivity and electrocatalytic performance.
Moreover, the hierarchical hollow and porous structure of 2D ZNCO nanosheets derived via solid leaves like Zn-Co-MOF, was further studied by TEM, HR-TEM and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) analysis. Fig. 4a and b depicts the lower and higher magnified TEM images of triangular leaf shaped hollow and loosely stacked ZNCO nanosheets, which was well-agreement with result of FESEM analysis. Also, the low magnification TEM image (Fig. 4a) showed the ultrathin rGO nanosheets anchored with ZNCO nanostructure. From the magnified view of TEM image (Fig. 4c), it was notably observed that hollow ZNCO nanostructure were composed of highly interconnected nanocrystallities creating inter-particle voids along with high porosity features to ZNCO nanosheets. HRTEM image of hollow ZNCO nanostructure was presented in Fig. 4d. As displayed in Figure, the lattice fringes with interplanar spacing of 0.244, 0.286 and $0.462~\mathrm{{nm}}$ were associated with (311), (222) and (111) crystal planes of ZNCO respectively (JCPDS No. 20-0781), which were perfectly coincided with XRD results [39,47,54]. The SAED pattern (inset of Fig. 4d) further verified the polycrystalline nature with cubic-spinel phase of ZNCO nanostructure.
The specific surface areas (SSAs) and pore size distributions (PSDS) of the as-fabricated CO, NCO and ZNCO were scrutinized from the nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms using Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) analysis as displayed in Fig. 5. The isotherms of CO, NCO and ZNCO nanostructures (Fig. 5a) displayed the typical type IV curve with noticeable hysteresis loops, affirming the presence of ample mesopores and superb distribution of pores throughout the surface of samples [25,39]. The CO, NCO and ZNCO nanomaterials exhibited the BET SSAs of 34, 81 and $95~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The high SSAs of hierarchical triangular ZNCO nanosheets provides the large numbers of electrochemical active sites and ameliorate ion–electron interaction, resulting the superlative electrochemical performance. Moreover, the pore size distributions of CO, NCO and ZNCO nanomaterials were investigated by applying the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) method. All the prepared samples exhibited the average pore diameter of $2-$
$40~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , manifesting the mesoporous features as displayed in Fig. 5b. Imperatively, for triangular ZNCO-nanosheets, majority of the pore diameter was in the range of $2\ensuremath{-}9\ensuremath{\mathrm{nm}}$ , implying the much admirable condition for electrochemical investigation using alkaline electrolyte. Additionally, the pore volume of ZNCO $\mathrm{\cdot}0.34\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ) was higher than those of NCO $(0.27~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ and CO $(0.098\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\$ . The presence of large pore volume reinforce the existence of paramount BET SSAs of MOFs derived ZNCO nanosheets. The upmost SSAs and enhanced pore volume of ZNCO nanosheets highly support for its outstanding electrochemical properties as well as most reliable evident to make it auspicious contender as electrode material for SCs applications.
## 3.2. Electrochemical investigation of positive electrodes in three electrode system
The overall outcomes achieved via structural and morphological aspects acknowledged that, novel hierarchical ZNCO nanosheet arrays were decorated onto rGO/NF by using 2D Zn-Co-MOF. The as-prepared triangular shaped hollow and porous ultrathin ZNCO nanosheets not only impart ample electroactive sites, fast-forth ion diffusion route, screening volume changes during charge–discharge process, rather also lavishly assure excellent electrical and mechanical contact with 3D rGO/NF substrate, nominating it as an auspicious electrode materials for energy storage and electrocatalysis applications. Primarily, the improvement in electrical conductivity of bare NF after decoration of rGO sheets was proven by showing the detailed electrochemical data in-terms of CV and GCD profiles along with Nyquist plot for both bare NF and rGONF (Fig. S6).
To assess the electrochemical activity for energy storage devices, the as-fabricated CO, NCO and ZNCO electrode was primarily examined as a supercapacitor electrode in a classic threeelectrode system. For this, CV, GCD and EIS measurements were conducted in 2 M KOH electrolytic solution using Pt coil as counter electrode, and $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ (3 M KCl) as reference electrode respectively. The comparative CV curves for CO, NCO and ZNCO at the scan rates of $50\ \mathrm{\mV}\ \mathrm{\s}^{-1}$ within the sweeping rate of $-0.2$ to $0.7\mathrm{V}$ were plotted in Fig. 6a, which explored the immense enclosed CV area for ZNCO, suggesting improved redox activity and excellent capacitive features compared to CO and NCO electrode [29,54]. Next, the representative CV plots for ZNCO electrode at the scan rates of $5{-}200~\mathrm{mV}~\ s^{-1}$ were presented in Fig. 6b. An obvious redox peaks were appeared from the CV results, disclosing pseudocapacitive features of electrodes were assigned for faradic reaction between $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}/\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}/{\mathsf{C o}}^{4+}$ with hydroxide $(\mathrm{OH^{-}})$ ions. On increasing sweep rate, both anodic and cathodic peaks for ZNCO increased linearly towards higher and lower potential respectively, without distortion on CV curve indicating minimal equivalent resistance and outstanding electrochemical reversibility of the electrode materials [55,56]. The reflection in CV curve during enhancement in sweep rate was governed by concentration as well as electrochemical polarization effect of electrode/electrolyte interface [57,58]. The CV profiles for CO and NCO electrodes at the identical sweep rate and potential windows were displayed in Figs. S7a and S8a. The appearance of oxidation and reduction peaks in CV curve was originated through faradic redox reactions and can be estimated as [24,26,39]:
$$
\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}+\mathrm{H_{2}O}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}\leftrightarrow3\mathrm{CoOOH}+\mathrm{e}^{-}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}+\mathrm{H_{2}O}+\mathrm{OH^{-}}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{NiOD}+\mathrm{2CoOH}+\mathrm{e^{-}}}\\ &{\mathrm{Ni(OH)_{2}}+\mathrm{OH^{-}}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{NiOOH}+\mathrm{H_{2}O}+\mathrm{e^{-}}}\\ &{\mathrm{Co_{2}O_{4}}^{-}+2\mathrm{H_{2}O}\leftrightarrow2\mathrm{CoOOH}+2\mathrm{OH^{-}}}\\ &{\mathrm{Co}(0\mathrm{H})_{2}+\mathrm{OH^{-}}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{CoOOH}+\mathrm{H_{2}O}+\mathrm{e^{-}}}\\ &{\mathrm{CoOOH}+\mathrm{OH^{-}}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{CoO_{2}}+\mathrm{H_{2}O}+\mathrm{e^{-}}}\\ &{\mathrm{ZnCo,O_{4}}+\mathrm{OH^{-}}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}}/\mathrm{OH^{-}}+\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}}-\mathrm{OH}}\end{array}
$$
The in-depth electrochemical investigation of the electrode materials were further explicated via GCD analysis. Fig. 6c depicts the pertinent GCD curves for CO, NCO and ZNCO electrodes were conducted in the potential range of $0.0{-}0.45\mathrm{~V~}$ at the current density of $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . The longest discharge time for ZNCO electrode declared the better specific capacitance compared to CO and ZNCO, which lavishly support the results of CV analysis. Fig. 6d discloses the GCD profile for ZNCO electrode at the lower to higher current densities of $1-50\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ . It turned out to be that, the GCD plateau were entirely nonlinear, manifesting pseudocapacitive behavior and charge storage mechanism was operated via quasi-reversible faradic process. Furthermore, the symmetrical nature of GCD profiles without any deviation even at the higher current densities (increased by 50 folds), were perfectly coincided with CV results and large enough to explain ultra-excellent rate performance of ZNCO electrode. Also, the GCD profile for CO and ZNCO electrodes were studied under the identical current densities and broadcasted in Figs. S7b and S8b, respectively. The ${\sf C}_{\sf S}$ values for individual electrodes at various current densities were estimated based on Eq. (4), and manipulated in Fig. 6e in the form of rate capability curve as well as in Table S1. As per the estimated data, the $C_{S}$ values for ZNCO electrode was achieved to be $267\:\mathrm{mAh}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was higher as than $C_{S}$ values for CO $\cdot124\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ ) and NCO $(219\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}.$ electrode at the current density of 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Surprisingly, when the current density was strengthened by 50 folds, ZNCO electrode still delivered ultra-high rate performance of $83.82\%$ to its initial ${\sf C}_{\sf S}$ value, which was much higher than those of CO $(\sim66.7\%)$ and NCO $(77.5\%)$ electrodes at the same current density. The ultra-high rate capability coupled with excellent $C_{s}$ value for ZNCO electrode could be ascribed to the following aspects; (i) Firstly, synergistic contribution of redox activity of Zn, Ni and Co metal provides plentiful electroactive sites for facile ion–electron transport enhancing the capacitance of ZNCO electrode, (ii) secondly, rGO support to uplift the conductivity of 3D Ni foam and reduce contact resistance providing active site for uniform growth of ZNCO nanomaterials, and (iii) next, MOF derived hollow ultrathin ZNCO nanosheets decorated with huge number of pores creating void space and large surface area, can provide and utilize myriad electroactive channels to promote facile core-penetration of electrolyte ions and ultrafast ion–electron transportation, thereby enhancing the capacitance as well as rate performance of ZNCO electrode. Undoubtedly, it should be advertised that, facile MOFs derived hierarchical ultrathin ZNCO electrode was furnished with magnificent capacity and rate performance as compared to formerly notified PTMOs based electrode materials, as administered in Table 1.
Aiming to enlighten the knowledge related to charge transfer kinetics and resistive properties of the as-prepared electrode materials, EIS test was executed and the responded Nyquist plots were displayed in Fig. 6f (inset shows the magnified segment of Nyquist plot). Basically, the exclusive resistive property of electrodes was interpreted in-depth taking two major detectable regions in Nyquist plot with semicircle portion at higher-frequency region and linear portion at low frequency region [24,38]. The point of intercept in EIS plot on the real impedance axis at higher frequency region is equivalent to resistance of solution $(\mathsf{R}_{s})$ , which was associated with contact resistance of current receiver/electrode interfaces plus the intrinsic resistance of electrode and electrolyte [5,47]. As broadcasted in Nyquist plot, the $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ values of ZNCO was grasped to be $_{0.36\ \Omega}$ which was lower than CO $\left(0.48~\Omega\right)$ and NCO $\left(0.4\Omega\right)$ electrodes, recommending the rapid charge discharge process and noteworthy electrical conductivity. Similarly, the radius of the semicircle portion at the frequency region signifies charge transfer resistance $\left(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}\right)$ , which was largely associated with ionic/electronic resistance originated through electrode/electrolyte interface [59,60]. The $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value for ZNCO was estimated to be $_{0.08\ \Omega}$ , which was much lower than $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ values for CO $\left(0.68~\Omega\right)$ and NCO $\left(0.12~\Omega\right)$ , implying the outstanding electrochemical performance of ZNCO in-terms of ultra-high rate capability and enhanced specific capacitance. Furthermore, the linear part of the Nyquist plot at lower frequency region signals the Warburg diffusion impedance (W), which basically signifies the diffusion and insertion process of electrolyte into electroactive surface of electrode [24,28]. The vertical line annexed for ZNCO electrode was much steeper than CO and NCO electrodes suggested the rapid diffusion and penetration of electrolyte ions towards the electrode surface, implying the excellent capacitive features of ZNCO electrode [23,47]. Essentially, the ultrathin, hollow and porous ZNCO nanosheet electrode exhibited the remarkable capacitive performance as strongly supported by minimal $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ and $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ values inclusive of substantial diffusion kinetics, which were well coincided with CV and GCD outputs under examination.
To access the practicability of supercapacitor device, cyclic lifespan of electrode materials was considered to be prominent factor.
The cyclic stability for all electrodes including CO, NCO and ZNCO were examined through consecutive 6000 GCD measurements at $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ and the achieved results were presented as the capacitance retention versus cycle numbers with respect to first cycle, as illustrated in Fig. 6g (inset shows the first and last 5 GCD cycles for CO, NCO and ZNCO). The ZNCO electrode revealed the outstanding life-span with $90.16\%$ capacitance retention even after consecutive 6000 GCD cycles, which was higher than those of CO $(79.45\%)$ and NCO $(83.67\%)$ electrodes. To further asses the charge transfer kinetics and electronic conductivity of ZNCO nanomaterials, EIS plots were displayed in Fig. S9 (inset shows the equivalent circuit model derived from EIS measurement). As shown in Fig., there was no obvious changes in $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ and $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ values after consecutive 6000 GCD cycles, exploring the outstanding electrochemical performances of ZNCO electrode. The enhanced cyclic stability of ZNCO electrode was attributed to the synergistic contribution of poly-phase transition metals and complete activation process of electrode leading to the presence of ample electrochemical active sites during cycling process.
The electrochemical kinetics as well as in-depth reaction kinetics of ZNCO electrode was analyzed via outcomes of CV test. Fig. 7a depicts the CV profile of ZNCO electrode at the various scan rate of $5{-}50\operatorname{mV}s^{-1}$ . On increasing the scan rates, the nature of CV curves were well-maintained without any distortion. The reaction kinetics of ZNCO electrode in-terms of capacitance could be examined interms of two aspects: (i) the capacitive controlled and (ii) the diffusion controlled process [61]. Fig. 7b provides well-fitted linear relationship between peak current versus square root of scan rate $(\nu^{1/2}).$ recommending the reaction kinetics was primarily governed via diffusion-controlled process and battery type-behavior of electrode material [45]. Furthermore, the degree of capacitive contribution was investigated by using power law as presented by following equation [1]:
$$
i=a\nu^{b}
$$
where i designates the current signal (A), $\upsilon$ represents the related scan rate $\mathsf{\Gamma}(\mathrm{m}\mathsf{V}\ s^{-1})$ , and $a$ and $b$ are adjustable constants. The bvalue can be achieved via the slope of log (i) vs log (v) based on following equation:
$$
\mathsf{l o g}(i)=\mathsf{l o g}a+\mathsf{b l o g}(\nu)
$$
Primarily, the b-value of 0.5 defines the diffusion-controlled faradic process, while b-value of nearby 1 is symbolic presentation for surface controlled or capacitive controlled redox process [18,45]. The b-value for ZNCO electrode was estimated to be 0.69 for anodic peaks under the scan rates of $5{-}50\operatorname{mV}s^{-1}$ (Fig. 7c), suggesting the dominance of diffusion-controlled faradic process over the total capacity and battery-type characteristic of ZNCO electrode. Consequently, the total current (i) in the CV profile at a constant potential (V) can be connoted as the amalgamation of the diffusion controlled $(k_{2}\nu^{1/2})$ and surface controlled $(k_{1}\nu)$ process, as advertised via following equation [23]:
$$
i(\mathsf{V})=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}
$$
The above equation can be expressed as:
$$
i(\mathsf{V})/\nu^{1/2}=k_{1}\nu^{1/2}+k_{2}
$$
According to Eq. (16), by plotting $i(\mathsf{V})/\nu^{1/2}$ versus $\nu^{1/2}$ , the adjustable constants, $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ can be achieved from the slope of the fitted line and $\mathsf{y}$ -axis intercept, respectively. Ultimately, the ratio of diffusion and surface-controlled faradic process over the total capacity of ZNCO electrode were calculated and displayed in Fig. 7d. As expected, the percentage of diffusion-controlled process was $59\%$ the lower scan rate of $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}\varsigma^{-1}$ . When the scan rate of CV profile was enhanced by 10 times $(5-50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}.$ , the percentage of diffusion-controlled process slightly decreased, while at the same time capacitive controlled process was increased from $41\%$ to $69\%$ , suggesting the dominance of surface-controlled redox reaction at higher scan rate and evidence for limited intercalation of ions onto surface of electrode [62]. Instead, at lower scan rate the diffusion controlled faradic process was highly responsible for high specific capacity of ZNCO electrode, induced via deep and feasible ion insertion towards electrode surface. Additionally, the optimal ratio of either diffusion or capacitive controlled mediated faradic process could be assigned for poor rate capability of electrode materials [62]. However, the recorded ultra-high rate capability of ZNCO electrode could be ascribed to synergetic contribution of both surface-controlled and diffusion-controlled process to redox reactions and kinetics of charge storage phenomena [23].
The superlative electrochemical performance of MOF derived ZNCO nanosheets grown onto 3D rGO/NF could be assigned to the following microstructural features as illustrated in Fig. 6e: (1) 3D rGO/NF serve as current collector with enhanced conductivity and macroporous features, which also tender the shorter iondiffusion roadway along with spontaneous path for flow of electrons; (2) the coupling effect of Zn, Ni and Co metal ions in a single framework provide sheer electroactive site with improved faradic reaction and conductive network as compared to their counterparts, which highly favors rapid ion–electron transportation thereby enhancing the capacitive performance along with overall electrochemical activity, (3) the MOF derived ultrathin hollow ZNCO nanosheet assumed to possess large surface area with highly porous nature which can manifests copious reactive sites and provide multichannel roadways for effortless transportation of solvated electrolyte ions and charge carriers thereby enhancing the diffusion kinetics of electrode material, (4) the extensive specific surface area of hierarchical ZNCO nanosheets tenders abundant electroactive sites for ultra-rapid electrochemical reactions with great reversibility, admirable electrode–electrolyte contact as well as minimal charge transfer resistance thereby elevating the electrochemical performance in-terms of enhanced capacity and exceptional rate performance, (5) the void space garnished within ZNCO nanosheets can bolster the volumetric alteration and respective intrinsic stress during successive charge/discharge cycles to preserve the electrode material from possible structural deterioration and slackening the electrical framework, and (6) the direct and uniform growth of ZNCO nanosheets on rGO/NF eschews the use of polymer binder and acquires better electronic contact between conductive substrate and ZNCO nanomaterials.
The ZNCO electrode decorated with enhanced specific capacitance value with ultrahigh rate performance and remarkable electrochemical durability for a large number of GCD cycles suggests that, it could be an assuring positive electrode for an HSC device for practical implementation. Similarly, for a negative electrode we have designed the MOF derived porous carbon integrated with chemically reduced rGO and denoted as rGO-MDPC according to our previously reported method [53]. The in-depth morphology (Fig. S10) electrochemical investigation (Fig. S11) for rGO-MDPC electrode were explained in supporting information.
## 3.3. Electrochemical performances of the as-assembled ZNCO//rGO-MDPC HSC device
In consideration of marvelous faradic behavior of the ZNCO and outstanding EDLC feature of rGO-MDPC electrodes, a HSC device $(\mathrm{ZNCO}//\mathrm{rGO-MDPC})$ with widen working potential was assembled by coupling these integral electrodes, 2 M KOH as electrolyte and cellulose filter paper as a separator, to survey its commercial applications (schematic representation for HSC device was provided in Fig. 8a). On the basis of specific capacity $(\mathsf C_{s})$ value and working voltage of complementary electrodes, the ideal mass loading proportion $\mathrm{(m_{+}/m_{-})}$ was evaluated to be 0.67. Furthermore, on the basis of working voltage for positive and negative electrodes the convenient potential for ASC device was selected to be $1.5{\mathrm{~V}}$ , the broadened potential window for HSC device was attributed to the synergistic contribution of faradic as well as EDLC electrode materials. The selection of appropriate voltage range was further supported by CV and GCD profiles of HSC device at the different voltage range (Fig. S12a–b). Fig. 8b depicts the CV measurements for respective positive (ZNCO) and negative (rGO-MDPC) electrodes in three electrode configuration at the scan rate of $20\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ with 2 M KOH, which graciously assists to procure the stable working voltage range for HSC device. Fig. 8c shows the CV curves accomplished for HSC device within the voltage range of $_{0-1.5\mathrm{~V~}}$ at multiple sweep rates of 5 to $200\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ and all the CV curves appeared strongly evidence the presence of highly reversible faradic behavior from the essential electrode materials. Additionally, no substantial alteration in the shape of CV profile was anticipated with enhancement in sweep rate, predicting the prudent rate performance as the conclusive of speedy and dynamic ion/electron transport kinetics towards the surface of electroactive materials [24,63]. The GCD measurements were conducted under the same voltage window at the current densities of $1-20\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 8d), and almost all GCD curve possess symmetric nature with respect to their charge–discharge halves, implying the high reversibility and prominent columbic efficiency of HSC device. The non-linear behavior of GCD profile were well-coincided with CV outcomes, affirming that charge storage phenomena in HSC device drives through faradic reactions. Also, there was no noticeable alteration in GCD curve even the current density was enhanced by 20 times, suggesting excellent rate performance of HSC device. The specific capacity $(\mathsf C_{\mathsf S})$ values for HSC device were evaluated at all current densities using Eq. (4) and advertised in Fig. 8e with respect to current density. The decay in $C_{S}$ values with mount in current density could be accredited to indigent diffusion of electrolyte ions, which block the exhaustive usage of electroactive station of electrode materials. The maximum $C_{S}$ value for HSC device was achieved to be $83.4~\mathrm{mAh}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , and surprisingly preserved the $60\%$ of initial capacity, even at the highest current density of $20\mathrm{~A~}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , signifying impressive rate performance.
The long-term cyclic stability is crucial factor for commercial activity of HSC device. Consequently, the cycling performance was examined via the GCD measurement at relatively higher current density of ${10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}}$ (Fig. 8f-inset shows the GCD cycles before and after the stability). It turned out to be that, HSC device suffered minimal decline of capacity retention $(<6\%)$ of its initial $C_{s}$ value even after consecutive 6000 GCD cycles, exploring outstanding long-term durability for practical application of $\mathsf{Z N C O}//\mathrm{rGO}-$ MDPC HSC device.
The charge transfer kinetics conjoined with intrinsic resistance and ion diffusion phenomena of HSC device before and after the cyclic life-span was appraised via EIS study in the form of Nyquist plot (Fig. $8\mathrm{g}.$ -inset depicts the magnified section as well as pertinent equivalent circuit diagram). It can be discerned from the Nyquist plot, the HSC device revealed there was no apparent alteration in both $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ and $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ values before and after the 6000 GCD cycles. After cycling, the $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ value was slightly changed (0.58–0.77 $\Omega$ ) along with minimal changes in $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ value $(1.94\mathrm{-}2.17\Omega)$ , featuring excellent electroactive performance of HSC device. Furthermore, there was no obvious transfiguration in vertical section of the slope at lower frequency region of Nyquist plot, demonstrating resemblance diffusion kinetics after successive cycles. After indepth electrochemical investigation, all the results implanted that ZNCO//rGO-MDPC HSC device disclosed perceptible capacitive performance, even after consecutive 6000 GCD cycles.
The energy and power densities of SCs are supposed to be imminent properties to propound the practicability of the HSC device and were computed from GCD profile using Eqs. (5) and (6) respectively. Fig. 8h depicts the corresponding Ragone plot (energy density vs power density plot) for ZNCO//rGO-MDPC HSC device, which showed the highest energy density 61.25 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at the power density of $750\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and still managed to deliver energy density of 35.93 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of 14372 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ . The estimated energy and power densities values for $\mathsf{Z N C O}//\mathrm{rGO}-$ MDPC HSC device eventuated from outstanding $C_{s}$ values and broadened working voltage were nearly comparable and superior to those of antecedently reported PTMOs and hydroxide based supercapacitor device as displayed in Table 2, affirming applicability of this HSC device. Ultimately, based on the remarkable energy storage capability and marvelous electrochemical activity, the energy and power source derived through ZNCO//rGO-MDPC HSC device were executed in real world electronic commercialization. The as-assembled two HSC devices when connected in a series, the working potential was prolonged up to ${\sim}3.0\mathrm{V}$ . Next, after being charged, these devices were enabled to power a red light-emitting diode (LED) bulb with galore intensity (Fig. 8i). These miraculous results strongly favor the suitability of the as-sandwiched $z_{\mathsf{N C O}//}$ rGO-MDPC HSC device as a propitious candidate for enhanced energy and power storage performer in the field of energy storage device.
## 3.4. Electrocatalytic activity towards methanol $(C H_{3}O H)$ oxidation
In this work, the electrolytic activity of MOF derived ZNCO electrode for methanol oxidation was investigated in brief for the first time. Fig. 9a displays the CV analysis of ZNCO electrode recorded at the scan rate of $20\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ using bare 1 M KOH and 1 M KOH plus 0.5 M $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ electrolytes. The CV profile for ZNCO electrode exhibited the well-consistency with the resulted CV curve for supercapacitor performance, suggesting the remarkable faradic property of electrode, which could flourish plentiful electroactive sites for electro-oxidation of methanol [6]. Instead, with the addition of $0.5~\mathrm{{M}}$ methanol into 1 M KOH electrolyte, the nature of CV curve was different and displayed much sharp increase in anodic peak current compared to that of bare $1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ , suggesting catalytic behavior of ZNCO electrode. Basically, regarding heterogeneous reactions, it was assumed that catalyst will absorb the reactants (methanol) onto its surface, followed by conversion of reactants into products by means of intermediate products formation [64,65]. Therefore, the electrocatalytic performance of ZNCO towards $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ oxidation was stipulated by nature of anodic peak in forward reaction, while the cathodic peak signifies the oxidation of intermediate species generated in forward reaction. The possible reaction mechanism behind the electrocatalytic activity of ZNCO in basic electrolyte can be ascribed as [66,67]:
$$
\mathrm{ZnOOH}+\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}+1.25\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{Zn(OH)}_{2}+\mathrm{CO}_{2}+1.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
$$
\mathrm{NiOOH}+\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}+1.25\mathrm{O}_{2}\longrightarrow\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}+\mathrm{CO}_{2}+1.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
$$
2C_{0}O O H+2C H_{3}O H+2.50_{2}\rightarrow2C_{0}(O H)_{2}+2C0_{2}+3H_{2}O
$$
Firstly Zn, Ni and Co metal atoms in ZNCO phase were oxidized to increased valence states in KOH electrolyte with the formation of ZnOOH, NiOOH and CoOOH respectively. Secondly, during the forward potential sweep, Zn, Ni and Co species with higher oxidation state were employed for oxidation of methanol and simultaneously reduced to lower oxidation states as explained by Eqs. (18)– (20). The disappearance of oxidation peak in forward direction could be ascribed to the suppression effect induced by oxidation of methanol [65]. In this study, ZnOOH, NiOOH and CoOOH primarily served as the active species for the oxidation of methanol and the electrocatalytic activity of ZNCO fabricated over rGO coated
Ni foam was attributed to the coupling effect of multiple valence states of Zn, Ni and Co atoms along with rGO decoration onto conductive substrate, which endowed composite effect, thereby upgrading the electronic conductivity of the fabricated ZNCO nanomaterials. As illustrated in the CV curve (Fig. 9a), the current density for ZNCO electrode was largely increased from $10.34\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ to $26\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ at the sweep rate of $20\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ after the addition of $0.5\mathrm{M}$ methanol to 1 M KOH solution, exhibiting its fabulous catalytic activity towards methanol electro-oxidation. Next, the onset potential of electrode material signifies pivotal role towards their electrocatalytic performance. It can be observed from Fig. 9a, the onset potential for ZNCO electrode was evaluated to be $0.27~\mathrm{V}$ which was much lower than previously reported electrocatalysts [65,68–70]. Instead, MOFs derived ZNCO nanomaterial was tested for the first time towards methanol elecctroxidation and is considered to be cost effective materials with excellent electrocatalytic activity. Consequently, there is still vast opportunity for electrocatalytic research related to ZNCO nanomaterials and their composite followed by its real-world applications.
The electrochemical stability of ZNCO catalyst for electrooxidation of methanol was scrutinized by chronoamperometry (it) curves at $0.7\mathrm{V}$ (vs Ag/AgCl) in presence of ( $1\mathrm{M}\mathrm{KOH}+0.5$ M $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ - OH) electrolyte for 2000 s (s) (Fig. 9b). Initially, owing to rapid polarization the decrement in current density $(25.72\mathrm{~A}/\mathrm{g}$ to 24.34 $\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g})$ was observed for a $57s_{\scriptscriptstyle{\cdot}}$ which might be ascribed to amassing of intermediate product that originated via adsorption and desorption of methanol towards the surface of electrodes showing poisoning the catalyst [67]. Nonetheless, ZNCO electrode still delivered ${\sim}95\%$ retention to its initial current density value, suggesting the outstanding stability as an electrocatalyst for methanol oxidation. The stability test was further investigated by using CV cycling test at the scan rate of $20\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , and the results were computed in Fig. 9c. The CV curve after cycling showed the reasonable stability with $94\%$ retention of its initial current density after consecutive 500 cycles. The decay in current density could be accredited to methanol utilization during cyclic test and proximate intermediate species formation during electrochemical reactions [6,69,71]. All these results emphasized that as-prepared ZNCO nanomaterials can assuredly acts as immensely stable and extremely reactive electrocatalyst for methanol oxidation.
The charge transfer property along with resistive features of ZNCO nanomaterial during electrocatalysis was scrutinized by EIS measurement in bare 1 M KOH as well as the ( $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}+0.5\ \mathrm{M}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\overline{{\mathrm{.}}}$ ) electrolytes. The related Nyquist plot for both the electrolytes were broadcasted in Fig. 9d, which exhibited unpretentious semicircle at higher frequency sector, announcing the minimal $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ value at the electrode–electrolyte interface. Additionally, the addition of $0.5\mathrm{M}$ methanol into KOH solution did not consort any alteration in the nature of Nyquist plot with only diminishment in slope of Warburg impedance, displaying the tremendous capacity of ZNCO to endure poisoning effect of intermediate products [6,65,72]. After consecutive $500\mathrm{CV}$ cycles there was an obvious change in $\mathtt{R}_{s}$ value but minimal changes in $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ value conjoint with slight decrement in slope of vertical line at the lower frequency region, suggesting the higher conductivity, desirable electrocatalytic activity and outstanding stability of ZNCO nanomaterials.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we have successfully prepared hierarchical ZNCO nanosheets arrays embellished with hollow and porous feature onto rGO/NF by MOFs derived solution based method followed by post-calcination approach. Benefiting from highly porous and ultrathin nature of hierarchical ZNCO nanosheet decorated over rGO/NF, it delivered excellent specific capacity value with recorded ultra-high rate performance and remarkable cyclic life. Imperatively, the assembled ZNCO//rGO-MDPC HSC device displayed exceptional energy density with optimal power density and superb cycling life. Also, the as-prepared ZNCO electrode exhibited lower onset potential, optimal current density and high cyclic stability for methanol electro-oxidation. Our work reveals that the hollow, porous and ultrathin ZNCO nanosheet arrays play a crucial role in advancing electrochemical and electrocatalytic properties. Thus, the present facile MOFs derived solution based method could be one of the best reference to develop mixed transition metal oxide/- graphene based composite electrode materials onto 3D conductive substrate with excellent electrochemical performance for future energy storage applications.
